{"hash":"353bf25acc1860f4","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":83.735,"said":"You have walked up Sannidhi Street, past the stalls of conch shells and the cans for carrying holy water home, and now the Rajagopuram rises before you, nine tiers and about fifty-three metres high. Who looked after this temple through the centuries? Let me introduce you to the Sethupathis. Their title means ‘lord of the Setu’, the bridge, and guarding it was their duty. Some four hundred years ago, in the early 1600s, the pilgrim road to Rameshwaram ran through wild country, and travellers were robbed on the way. So Muthu Krishnappa Nayak, the king of Madurai, made a local chief named Sadaikka Thevar the first recorded Sethupathi, to protect the pilgrims and keep the road safe. His family ruled from Ramanathapuram, across the strait on the mainland, and generation after generation they gave this temple land, halls and towers. Others had built here before them: the Pandya kings of Madurai, and a king of Jaffna, in Sri Lanka, who sent stone across the sea for the sanctum. But it was the Sethupathis who shaped the temple you are about to enter, and their greatest gift lies just inside. Step through the gate with me.\n\nDid you know? The temple stands on about fifteen acres, and its outer wall runs roughly 865 feet from east to west.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1131},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1147,"end":1248}],"words":[[0.098,0.168,0,3],[0.278,0.168,4,8],[0.459,0.3,9,15],[0.771,0.156,16,18],[0.939,0.998,19,35],[2.141,0.337,36,40],[2.49,0.096,41,44],[2.598,0.409,45,51],[3.019,0.132,52,54],[3.163,0.337,55,60],[3.512,0.397,61,67],[3.92,0.144,68,71],[4.076,0.072,72,75],[4.161,0.361,76,80],[4.533,0.132,81,84],[4.677,0.421,85,93],[5.11,0.325,94,98],[5.447,0.349,99,104],[5.807,0.361,105,109],[6.372,0.192,111,114],[6.576,0.192,115,118],[6.781,0.108,119,122],[6.901,0.733,123,134],[7.646,0.469,135,140],[8.127,0.397,141,147],[8.536,0.264,148,151],[9.028,0.325,153,1